{"name":"Lifeboat Blue","hex":"#81b6bc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lifeboat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A boat especially designed for saving the lives of shipwrecked people or people in distress at sea (either launched from the shore with a crew, or else carried on board a larger ship).","An emergency vehicle carried aboard a spaceship."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To rescue; to carry to safety"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lifeboat"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lifeboat (shipboard)","blocks":[{"text":"A lifeboat or liferaft is a small, rigid or inflatable boat carried for emergency evacuation in the event of a disaster aboard a ship. Lifeboat drills are required by law on larger commercial ships. Rafts (liferafts) are also used. In the military, a lifeboat may double as a whaleboat, dinghy, or gig. The ship's tenders of cruise ships often double as lifeboats. Recreational sailors usually carry inflatable liferafts, though a few prefer small proactive lifeboats that are harder to sink and can be sailed to safety."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Brosen_lifeboats_scandinavia.jpg/330px-Brosen_lifeboats_scandinavia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lifeboat_(shipboard)","wikidata":"Q1746393"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Portland_Pudgy_proactive_lifeboat.jpg/330px-Portland_Pudgy_proactive_lifeboat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Portland_Pudgy_proactive_lifeboat.jpg/330px-Portland_Pudgy_proactive_lifeboat.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Proactive lifeboat-safety dinghy for recreational cruisers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APortland_Pudgy_proactive_lifeboat.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Collision_of_Costa_Concordia_24.jpg/500px-Collision_of_Costa_Concordia_24.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Collision_of_Costa_Concordia_24.jpg/1280px-Collision_of_Costa_Concordia_24.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Collision_of_Costa_Concordia_24.jpg/500px-Collision_of_Costa_Concordia_24.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th